Keep a Qwen3-VL vision tower's transformer blocks out of VRAM, so the KV pool
gets the bytes. Stock vLLM 0.27.1 cannot do this at all.

Two backends, selected by VLLM_VISION_OFFLOAD_BACKEND (or VISION_OFFLOAD= in
single-user/start_qwen.sh):

  prefetch  RECOMMENDED. Weights live in pinned host RAM; each block is
            streamed H2D into a small GPU staging pool just before it runs,
            overlapped with the previous block's compute. Measured: +0.72 GiB
            of KV pool, and free (within noise) on images >= 896px.
  uva       Weights live in pinned host RAM and the GPU reads them across PCIe
            *inside every GEMM* (zero-copy, no staging). Measured: +0.78 GiB --
            marginally more, since there is no staging buffer -- but 2.8-4.8x
            on image latency.
  off       Upstream behavior (default). NB: at MAX_LEN=140000 this deployment
            will not even START with the tower resident (see below).

Why stock vLLM can't do either: --cpu-offload-gb / --cpu-offload-params are
accepted without error but are a silent NO-OP for vision towers. The
offloader's only call site in the entire codebase is
`get_offloader().wrap_modules(...)` inside make_layers()
(model_executor/models/utils.py), and make_layers() builds the *language model*
decoder stack only (qwen3_5.py's Qwen3_5Model). The vision tower builds its
blocks with a plain `nn.ModuleList` (qwen3_vl.py's Qwen3_VisionTransformer), so
the offloader never sees them. Symptom: no "Total CPU offloaded parameters"
line is logged and the KV pool shrinks by the full weight of the tower.
Confirmed by grep: `wrap_modules` appears at exactly one call site.

Note the *filter* would have worked -- "visual" is segment-exact (the matcher
is `f".{param}." in f".{name}."`) and matches exactly the 333 model.visual.*
tensors and zero language-model tensors on this checkpoint. It is only the
call-site coverage that is missing.

Both backends use a *dedicated* offloader instance rather than the global one,
so this budget can neither be consumed by nor steal budget from language-model
layers: for uva, cpu_offload_params is left empty (= "offload everything handed
to me") and the only modules handed over are the ViT blocks.

Only the 27 transformer blocks are offloaded (0.7664 of the tower's 0.8582
GiB). patch_embed, pos_embed, merger and deepstack_merger_list stay in VRAM
(0.0917 GiB): they are small and, unlike the blocks, are not a repeating unit
whose staging buffers a slot pool could share. (On this checkpoint
deepstack_visual_indexes is [], so deepstack_merger_list is empty.)

Safe here because the ViT is neither torch.compile'd nor cudagraph'd in this
deployment (compile_mm_encoder and cudagraph_mm_encoder are both False). Do not
assume that holds if those are enabled -- see the _VisionPrefetchOffloader
docstring, which depends on it directly.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE PREFETCH BACKEND

Mechanically it reuses vLLM 0.27.1's own PrefetchOffloader
(model_executor/offloader/prefetch.py): _CpuParamOffloader copies each param to
pinned host memory at *construction* time and frees the VRAM immediately --
before load_weights, which then writes into the CPU tensors, the same ordering
make_layers() already relies on. A StaticBufferPool allocates `prefetch_step`
slots of each unique (name, shape, stride, dtype); block N uses slot
N % prefetch_step. A dedicated copy stream does the H2D with per-layer events,
so block N's compute waits only on block N's copy, and start_onload_to_static()
forks off an event recorded on the compute stream so a copy can never overwrite
a buffer whose reads are still enqueued.

THREE HAZARDS, all of which this patch has to work around. Every one of them
fails SILENTLY -- wrong pixels, not an exception -- so validate with
test_vision_prefetch_offload.py, never by eyeballing generated text.

1. THE STOCK HOOKS ROUTE THROUGH THE GLOBAL SINGLETON. PrefetchOffloader's
   _hook_module_forward does not call itself -- it calls
   torch.ops.vllm.wait_prefetch / start_prefetch, and those resolve the
   offloader via the *global* get_offloader() (offloader/prefetch_ops.py:33,61).
   A dedicated instance is invisible to them: they hit the global
   NoopOffloader, whose _wait_for_layer / _start_prefetch are `pass`. That is
   NO SYNCHRONIZATION AT ALL, so the compute stream reads staging buffers the
   copy stream is still filling. _VisionPrefetchOffloader therefore overrides
   _hook_module_forward to call through the instance. Measured as the negative
   control in the test: 0/10 runs bit-exact, max abs err 490.6.
   Bypassing the custom ops is only safe because the ViT is not
   compiled/cudagraphed; their sole purpose is to give torch.compile a data
   dependency it cannot reorder.

2. THE STOCK WRAP-AROUND PREFETCH ALIASES A SLOT THAT IS STILL IN USE. Stock
   _hook_module_forward ends each block with
   `_start_prefetch((index + prefetch_step) % len(modules))`, pre-loading the
   head of the stack for the next pass. That is only correct when
   len(modules) % prefetch_step == 0. Otherwise the wrapped prefetch targets a
   slot a LATER block of the SAME pass still needs. With this tower's 27 blocks
   and prefetch_step=2: block 25 prefetches block 0 into slot 0, but block 26
   also uses slot 0 and its weights were staged back at block 24 -- so block 26
   computes on block 0's weights.
   Measured, 27 blocks / dim 1152 / 2048 tokens:
       27 blocks, step 2  ->  0/6 bit-exact (max abs err 2.172)   <-- the bug
       27 blocks, step 3  ->  6/6 bit-exact  (3 divides 27)
       26 blocks, step 2  ->  6/6 bit-exact  (2 divides 26)
   End to end it was visible as a single misread character in an OCR
   transcription -- exactly the kind of thing that reads as "the model is a bit
   weak at OCR" rather than as a bug.
   Fix: never wrap. The hook prefetches only while index + step < len, and
   prime() re-stages the head of the stack at the top of every forward. That is
   correct for any block count and any prefetch_step. Verified 6/6 bit-exact at
   27/step2, 27/step3, 27/step4 and 26/step2, and 10/10 at 27/step2 with 4096
   tokens.

3. post_init() NEVER RUNS FOR A DEDICATED INSTANCE. It allocates the staging
   pool, calls sync_cpu_storage() (picking up anything
   process_weights_after_loading changed) and repoints params at GPU buffers.
   The stock call site (gpu_model_runner.py:5572, after load_model) drives only
   the global offloader. Without it the params are still CPU tensors and the
   first ViT forward dies on a device mismatch. This patch calls it lazily from
   Qwen3_VisionTransformer.forward, which is after weight loading by
   construction and lands on the profiling run's dummy image encode -- so the
   staging pool is counted in the profiled peak and correctly excluded from the
   KV pool instead of overcommitting it. Confirmed in the startup log: the
   "Initialized 27 modules" line appears ~3 minutes before "Available KV cache
   memory".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
MEASURED (2026-08-25, this box: RTX 3090 @250 W, PCIe 4.0 x16, i7-6700K,
Qwen3.5-27B W4A16, CTX=long / fp8 KV, --max-num-seqs 8, MTP spec decode,
--limit-mm-per-prompt image 1024x1024 + --mm-processor-kwargs max_pixels
1048576, so the "1280" row below is really a 1024px image.)

Tower size, from the checkpoint's safetensors headers (333 model.visual.*
tensors, all BF16 -- the ViT is in the quantizer's 206-entry `ignore` list, so
it is unquantized even though the LM is W4A16):

  blocks (27)                324 tensors   0.7664 GiB   29.07 MiB/block
  merger                       6 tensors   0.0835 GiB
  patch_embed + pos_embed      3 tensors   0.0082 GiB
  TOTAL                      333 tensors   0.8582 GiB

The offloader reports exactly that: "Total GPU memory saved: 0.8227 GB, Static
buffer pool: 0.0609 GB" (= 0.7662 GiB offloaded, 0.0567 GiB staged).

KV pool:

  backend    pool       tokens    @140k   note
  off        5.18 GiB   --        --      REFUSES TO START at MAX_LEN=140000:
                                          "5.34 GiB KV cache is needed, which
                                          is larger than the available KV cache
                                          memory (5.2 GiB) ... estimated
                                          maximum model length is 136000"
  off        5.18 GiB   132,680   1.02x   at MAX_LEN=130000
  prefetch   5.90 GiB   153,592   1.10x
  uva        5.96 GiB   154,951   1.11x

So on this config the choice is not "vision costs some KV pool" -- with the
tower resident the server does not come up at all at the configured context
length. Run-to-run variation on the profiled pool is ~±0.03 GiB (a second
prefetch run measured 5.93 GiB / 154,951; a second uva run 5.99 / 156,310), so
treat the prefetch-vs-uva gap as ~0.06 GiB / ~1,400 tokens -- which is just the
staging pool, as expected.

Image latency, median of 5 unique images per size (bench/vision_offload_ab.py;
every image is unique so neither the mm-processor cache nor the prefix cache
can serve a repeat). End-to-end request time including 8 output tokens, so the
fixed ~0.15 s of prefill+decode is in every number:

  size    vis_tok   off      prefetch          uva
   224px       85   0.185s   0.232s (+0.047)   0.527s (2.8x)
   448px      217   0.286s   0.330s (+0.044)   1.380s (4.8x)
   896px      805   0.831s   0.835s (+0.004)   2.553s (3.1x)
  1024px     1045   1.075s   1.068s (-0.007)   3.824s (3.6x)

prefetch costs a fixed ~45 ms on small images and nothing measurable at 896px
and above -- i.e. exactly the ~41 ms it takes to pull 0.7664 GiB across a
~20 GB/s link once. On a small image the ViT has too little compute to hide it;
by 896px each block's compute (~30 ms) dwarfs its 1.5 ms of transfer. For the
OCR case -- full-page images -- streaming is free.

uva's cost instead SCALES with image size, and is far worse, because GEMM
kernels tile: a weight tile is re-read once per row-block of the activation
matrix, so a forward pass reads each weight many times. A 1024px image is
~4,096 patches, so the (1152 x 3456) qkv weight is re-read on the order of 30x
per layer. In VRAM (~936 GB/s) those re-reads are nearly free; across PCIe they
dominate. L2 (6 MB on a 3090) absorbs some but not most of it. The prefetch
backend sidesteps this entirely by reading each weight exactly ONCE per image,
into VRAM, where the re-reads run at full bandwidth.

The general lesson: UVA weight offloading suits memory-bound, low-arithmetic-
intensity layers, not a compute-heavy ViT fed large activation matrices.
Streaming suits anything whose weights are read a bounded number of times.

Choosing a backend:
  - prefetch (recommended): ~1,400 KV tokens worse than uva, ~60x cheaper on
    latency at 1024px. VLLM_VISION_PREFETCH_STEP=3 buys a block more slack
    against PCIe contention for 29.1 MiB more staging.
  - uva: only if you want the last ~0.06 GiB and images are rare enough that
    +0.3-2.7 s each does not matter. Set VLLM_VISION_CPU_OFFLOAD_GB > 0.
  - off: only below ~136k context on this box.
Capping --limit-mm-per-prompt's width/height also directly bounds the worst
case, since both backends' overhead scales with patch count.

Host RAM: the pinned 0.7664 GiB is not free on a 32 GB box that also gives
20 GiB to the offload connector's CPU KV tier. Consider dropping
cpu_bytes_to_use by ~1 GiB when enabling this.

NOT YET MEASURED: image latency under concurrent long-context load. The offload
connector moves KV blocks over the same PCIe link, and contention is the main
remaining risk to the prefetch backend's overlap. prefetch_step=3 is the
mitigation if it bites.

